{"text": " Now, when you open up Microsoft Co-Pilot, Auto is the default model, but Co-Pilot also has a model picker that lets you switch between different underlying models. So in other words, using Co-Pilot doesn't always mean working with the same AI model. Now, some of these models come from OpenAI, which is the company behind ChatGBT, and others come from Entropic, which is the company behind Cloud. Microsoft then brings them into Co-Pilot so that eligible users can access models from both companies within a familiar tool. Then exactly which one you see really depends on your organizational's license, settings, and also Microsoft rollout schedule, because these change all the time. Most likely, what you'll see is GBT 5.6 Quick Response, which is available across Co-Pilot Chat and Microsoft 365 apps like Word and Excel and PowerPoint. Co-Pilot can show you more than one GBT generation at once as Microsoft introduces newer models. You could also see GBT 6, which is a newer OpenAI model. Microsoft announced this rollout to Co-Pilot Chat, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and other experiences in September, 2026, and then Cloud Opus 5.5, which is a newer and tropic model joining Co-Pilot in that same rollout. Entropic reports improvements in knowledge work and writing over the previous Opus version, and if your organization has enabled those in tropic models and Opus 5.5 may become another option alongside OpenAI's models. Now, you don't need to remember every model name or version number, because they will change. But the important shift here is that Co-Pilot can bring together several leading models into the same work environment and that the model picker shows you which choices are available to you.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 1.2600000000000022, "end": 3.82, "text": " Now, when you open up Microsoft Co-Pilot,", "tokens": [50364, 823, 11, 562, 291, 1269, 493, 8116, 3066, 12, 47, 31516, 11, 50566], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19679740071296692, 